PASS in 182 s (`qa\u002d8b533d76ec\u002dwebdav\u002da\u002d20260915\u002d2321`). Build 5 is nine of nine and the device candidate (H700 `h700\u002dall\u002d20260915\u002d8b533d76ec`, tar sha `451d58190365b9f5\u2026`, not staged). Rule added to `generic\u002dx64\u002dvm\u002dtesting.md`: one writer per QA cloud folder while the suites run.\n\nTARGET 56 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_16\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 49bfac7ee3af7cc291d3dab0f0850a4df85903d8 96f373260b75805863807ebfd83d18a32abd2b04e93e317300ba576f82a952ae RANGES [[78, 81]] MASKED []\n78: ## 23:45 UTC \u2014 Build 9 nine of nine; the candidate for the RG SP\n79: \n80: \u002d Suites on `e287f9d21a`: nine PASSED (`qa\u002de287f9d21a\u002dwebdav\u002da\u002d20260916\u002d2316`, 23:16\u002d23:41 UTC); time\u002dto\u002dplay 0.88 / 1.43 / 1.51 s. QA rows for builds 8 and 9 in `docs/vm\u002dqa\u002dlog.md`. #205: the VM\u002dside criteria ticked, the rescan criterion superseded by D\u002dCLOUD\u002d132 in the body, the proofs and the frame correction in comments. H700 `h700\u002dall\u002d20260916\u002de287f9d21a` is the candidate; the RG SP was off the network (both addresses) when the run ended, so the staging question waits for the device.\n\nTARGET 57 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_17\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 5199da7047f37a41e26e55bd0fa9f4fd5acf3c33 eb9d83e4eace7ddf82fe5a49ac259d6a7ec82fc014e431c0d6c554aea2d3fbb9 RANGES [[145, 148]] MASKED []\n145: ## 20:50 UTC \u2014 #152 closed; the French needs no device look\n146: \n147: \u002d Maintainer: *\"Do you need me to switch the language? Can't you check whether or not it's French on the VM?\"* \u002d\u002d yes, and it did: the same binary, theme and 640x480 panel on guest d, the two pages framed on build 15, the catalogue held at 661 / 661 by the `french` suite on every build. #152 closed as delivered; the device box I had left for their read was not owed (the VM is the evidence, the device the confirmation only where the VM cannot reach \u002d\u002d a real panel's rendering of the same 640x480 layout is not such a case).\n\nTARGET 58 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_18\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY b2793efb499005742a97c4e984da3f28a493dfe6 333989b678036d9065290f41db1d27f3bbe9378ed830e511d8b544dc57b06661 RANGES [[218, 264]] MASKED [259]\n218: ## 18:00 UTC \u2014 #211 does not reproduce on the VM; the fixture pool grew from one achievement to three games\n219: \n220: **Three new fixtures, each verified by hash rather than by name.** Combo Fishing (GBC, itch),\n221: Cookie Clicker (NES, the author's own page) and MeteoRain (GBA, itch), chosen with the new\n222: `tools/ra\u002dcandidate\u002dgames`: RetroAchievements prefixes homebrew titles `~Homebrew~` and publishes\n223: per\u002dachievement unlock rates, so \"two achievements nearly every player holds\" is a query, not a\n224: guess \u2014 497 homebrew sets across the systems we ship, 351 with two or more achievements at 75%+.\n225: \n226: Two traps, both caught by checking the artifact:\n227: \n228: \u002d **The build matters.** Combo Fishing's itch page offers a 2024 and a 2026 build; RA knows only\n229:   the 2026 one. The 2024 file launches perfectly and would simply never fire an achievement.\n230: \u002d **The hash rule matters.** I declared Cookie Clicker \"NOT recognised\" on a whole\u002dfile md5. rcheevos\n231:   strips the 16\u002dbyte iNES header for NES, and the headerless hash is exactly the set's. I nearly\n232:   discarded the best candidate in the list over my own error. `\u002d\u002dcheck` now applies the per\u002dconsole\n233:   rule (iNES 16, SNES copier 512, the rest whole) so it is a command rather than a judgement.\n234: \n235: **#211 did not reproduce.** Cookie Clicker's route \u2014 `up` \u00d7 8, then `x`, awards at 1/50/100/500/1000\n236: cookies \u2014 drove **three** offline awards in one session and then **two** more in a second session\n237: with `cheevos_auto_screenshot = \"1\"`, the device's setting. RetroArch survived all five;\n238: `Segmentation|coredump` count zero. The conditions were matched one at a time: offline through the\n239: proxy, the set served from cache (`Set 5513: 10/10 achievements active`, and `3 of 10 unlocked` on\n240: relaunch from the queue), and **the badges cached and served** (70831/70842/70841/70840, 6048\u002d6384\n241: bytes) rather than 404 \u2014 which is the device's condition after #213's audit corrected this issue.\n242: \n243: So it is not the second award as such, not the badge fetch, and not the screenshot. What is left:\n244: the **core** (fbneo on the device against the image's NES core here), the architecture, and the\n245: load. The next experiment is the same route under fbneo, which needs an arcade ROM with a set \u2014\n246: asked of the maintainer rather than assumed.\n247: \n248: **Two costs of my own decisions, recorded so they are not repeated:**\n249: \n250: 1. **Letting the queue flush spent Cookie Clicker's cheap tier.** `Flush complete: total=5\n251:    flushed=5 pending_remaining=0` is a genuine end\u002dto\u002dend proof of the offline\u2192online path with\n252:    five awards on a new game \u2014 and 67124\u002d67128 are now earned, so that route cannot be driven\n253:    again. A fixture meant to be re\u002drun should have its queue cleared offline instead. Written into\n254:    the fixtures README and D\u002dRA\u002d006.\n255: 2. **740 clicks went nowhere** because 8 `up` presses from a *relaunch* put the cursor above the\n256:    cookie, and a miss is silent. The counter on screen is the ground truth; I should have read a\n257:    frame after the first batch rather than the eighth.\n258: \n259: [MASKED sensitive\u002dlabelled line; not read unredacted]\n260: no `key=` (the old call returns http 200 with `{\"errors\":[\"invalid key\"]}` as the body, so it fails\n261: as \"no downloads found\"), and a CDN path with no filename saved MeteoRain as `4113057`, invisible\n262: to EmulationStation. And a new ROM needs `essway.service` restarted before `POST /launch` will\n263: launch it \u2014 the POST returns 200 either way.\n\nTARGET 59 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_19\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY edb7b0c93a0ac5ef5aecd1f02085748e42a2b020 32e9e6f8a6e18b90160357f5571b4ca263e6e3e3f30d68d5e9833bf60517ad78 RANGES [[34, 69]] MASKED []\n34: ## 02:10 UTC \u2014 The RAMD playbook's twelve principles, mapped against ours\n35: \n36: The maintainer asked to adapt the twelve principles from the Awecelot RAMD\n37: playbook into our instruction files. Checked rather than assumed: **ten of the\n38: twelve already exist here under our own names**, spread across\n39: `engineering\u002dpractices.md`, `decision\u002dregister.md`, `issue\u002dtracking.md`,\n40: `instruction\u002dfiles.md`, `worktrees.md` and `vm\u002dfirst.md`.\n41: \n42: So the adaptation is an index, not a rewrite \u2014 `working\u002dprinciples.md`, one\n43: line per principle pointing at the rule that does the work. Their own\n44: hub\u002dand\u002dspoke rule argues for exactly this: spokes reference, never duplicate.\n45: Every pointer in it was verified against the actual headings before committing.\n46: \n47: **Two were genuinely missing and are written out:**\n48: \n49: \u002d *Urgency increases rigour.* Nothing in the corpus said it, and today is the\n50:   evidence: four claims made without checking, in one subsystem, while moving\n51:   fast toward an RC. The artifact check is the last thing to drop, not the\n52:   first; a deadline narrows scope, it does not lower the evidence standard.\n53: \u002d *Pre\u002dflight reading.* `instruction\u002dfiles.md` says which copy to read and that\n54:   a stale worktree lies; it never said to read **before starting**. Now it\n55:   does, with the registers included and a requirement to say which rules were\n56:   read \u2014 because a rule consulted and a rule assumed produce identical output.\n57: \n58: **One import is worth more than the rest: enforcement depth.** A rule goes\n59: written \u2192 routed \u2192 evidenced \u2192 audited \u2192 self\u002dexamining. Most of ours sit at\n60: written\u002dand\u002drouted, which is where decay happens (D\u002dUI\u002d039 lost 30 screens in a\n61: month). What holds is audited: `pkgcheck`, `register\u002dcheck`, `es\u002dmenu\u002dmap\u002dcheck`,\n62: `vm\u002dqa`, `pre\u002dpush`. So the standing response to a rule broken twice is to move\n63: it up a stage, not to rewrite it.\n64: \n65: **One not adopted:** *creation over destruction*, a cultural stance for an\n66: estate building products. Our equivalent instincts are already covered, and the\n67: useful half \u2014 declaring what we deliberately do not do \u2014 exists as\n68: `es\u002dmenu\u002dmap.md`'s `## Not mapped`.\n\nTARGET 60 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_20\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY fe698e53e17f78b4586e05e263b59961c4aed8ad 889c7b8d519ffd4523631e2eec135844c813a1e2a1e4e2cfddb6e357cfbe48b6 RANGES [[287, 308]] MASKED [306]\n287: ## 23:55 UTC \u2014 Frames on the RC: #160 and #94 done; two \"frames owed\" were not\n288: \n289: Guest b (1280x800) on `77e7e97515`, the QA RetroAchievements account carried\n290: in by `tools/qa\u002daccounts`, B\u00f6bl through a gamelist `cheevosId` of 4902: the\n291: achievements page frame has the header lines left\u002daligned, values in one\n292: column, bar and percentage beside the block, list below \u2014 #160's 1280x800\n293: box ticked (`docs/qa\u002dframes/2026\u002d09\u002d20/`). Three misfires on the way, each\n294: a fixture's: `scp \u002dp` is not the port flag (`\u002dP` is), the `ssh \u002dn` helper\n295: closes stdin so a heredoc never arrives, and a *restarted* ES lands on the\n296: last game list (memory: reboot the guest, or send B first) \u2014 a reboot put\n297: the carousel on NES as `