to this candidate\n334: (D\u002dQA\u002d032 says an ES change found during the round is made before the RG SP)\n335: or after the round. Also for the soak: keep Wi\u002dFi on until the row says\n336: COMPLETED, since a fetch that fails offline ends the scan as COULDN'T FINISH.\n\nTARGET 62 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_22\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY da1b8e3ddcf6adbfeaa9cb7e8ac12d4137484354 3f441615263ad35685c14df50b880491375b6d378c737ffa91a4402d06176440 RANGES [[233, 236]] MASKED []\n233: ## 23:50 UTC \u2014 #245 built on our side (D\u002dUI\u002d081): a game's captures turned by the rotation the display gave its frame\n234: \n235: The maintainer: *\"I think we do it on our side. If we want to file a bug upstream against RetroArch around it, we can do that, but I imagine RetroArch moves much more slowly than we will.\"* D\u002dUI\u002d081 settled (was open). The interface can turn a picture as easily as it fits it; what it lacked was the angle, a per\u002dgame fact the core states at launch (`[Environ] SET_ROTATION: \"n\"` in `/var/log/exec.log`). So it learns it: `CaptureRotation::recordAfterSession` runs where play count and last played are updated, reads the log's last line, folds `video_allow_rotate` and the player's `video_rotation` from RetroArch's config (`CaptureRotationText`, unit\u002dtested: log, config, record text), and writes `<savestates>/<system>/<rom>.rotation` \u002d\u002d beside the states, so it travels with them; a game never played here has no record and no captures. `DisplayAspect::Transform` (aspect + turns) replaces the bare ratio and is read by every surface that draws a capture: the SAVE STATE MANAGER's tiles (grid\u002dwide), the SCREENSHOTS list's picture (the theme's bound extra and md_image), the full\u002dscreen viewer (`ZoomableImageComponent`) and its grid, and the SCREENSHOTS system in grid style (`ImageGridComponent::setTileDecorator`, a per\u002dtile hook, since each tile is another game's). `ImageComponent::setDisplayRotation` turns the picture inside its layout box \u002d\u002d the texture corners move round, the quad stays; an odd turn swaps the fitted width and height and the min\u002dtarget crop's axis. The direction: RetroArch's rotation 1 is 90 degrees counter\u002dclockwise, so the raw frame's right edge is the game's top; the fixture carries a green mark on that edge and the proof asks where the mark lands. ES `12de21757`, pin `a4c06e89a`, tenth cut `bf53cea7e4` building; the VM proof seeds `Bobl.rotation`/`Ninoid.rotation` = 1 and a session of cookieclicker with a SET_ROTATION line appended to the launch log for the recording path (`seed\u002d245.sh`, `proof\u002d245s.sh`, `exit\u002drecord\u002d245.sh`). Unit suite 118/1281. Left as they are and said so on #245: the screensaver slideshow and a game image set by hand to a screenshot.\n\nTARGET 63 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_23\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY f7490fa5d0a90258bea1efe1146cee7eab39b98d 50423570f38ec61487f7fb49baf4523ce8e054afbf00aac00b7b1642c4024387 RANGES [[95, 98]] MASKED [97]\n95: ## 23:55 UTC \u2014 The sixteenth cut's batch built: #209, #198, #247 (ES `75ca1dac2`)\n96: \n97: [MASKED sensitive\u002dlabelled line; not read unredacted]\n\nTARGET 64 docs/work\u002dlogs/2026_09\u002dwork_logs/2026_09_24\u002dwork_log.md bounded\u002dday\u002dentry\nIDENTITY 84f867de4b2928527a039d38daf5eafc9c82181d b9dae60d61cec4f8da04a81350d125fdef3ce7799eeda3fa16d2686c3c2b8a22 RANGES [[126, 131]] MASKED []\n126: ## 23:20 UTC \u2014 Bucket B re\u002dderived: 17 of the 35 \"device\" boxes were already the VM's, 10 more can be, 6 name a physical fact (#266, D\u002dQA\u002d043 open)\n127: \n128: The maintainer, on the twenty\u002dsecond cut's device questions: *\"With 35 issues waiting on my word on a device, do those actually need me on the device? Are we really checking to see if any of those can be answered on the VM and automated? Going through 35 items on a device is pretty onerous.\"* Every open box of the 35 read again and set beside the frames under `docs/qa\u002dframes/`: seventeen issues (#65 #66 #67 #68 #69 #82 #94 #157 #160 #190 #194 #202 #203 #208 #210 #243 #245 #250 \u002d\u002d #203's box is the VM's own) have the behaviour framed at the panel's size already, and their device box is a re\u002dobservation; ten (#54 #55 #64 #113 #179 #188 #198 #211 #213 #249) the VM can show with a walk, a suite case, a fault\u002dinjecting backend mode or the QA account, two with P\u002d05's synthetic\u002dinput note; six name a fact the guest cannot have (two units on a LAN, the H700 watchdog \u002d\u002d a change, mis\u002dfiled \u002d\u002d the hotspot drop, Tailscale on a battery restart, the soak, the Nova), and four of those are journal reads after the maintainer's own use. #200's fourteen and #236 \u00a7 A's \"You\" boxes collapse to the same four. Filed as #266 with the table and the acceptance criteria; D\u002dQA\u002d043 opened (a VM observation at the panel's size is the observation; a confirmation is a comment, never a criterion), home #266.\n129: \n130: Why it happened: the boxes predate `vm\u002dfirst.md` or were written as a courtesy confirmation after a VM proof, and the triage (#262) grouped the boxes by what they ask rather than re\u002dderiving each against the evidence \u002d\u002d blindspot 51 at bucket scale. The guard proposed on #266 is a lint on a box that says \"the maintainer's word\" or \"on the H700\" with no physical fact beside it.\n"
